//! run
//! abort
//! link: ffi/oracle.c
//! stderr: ft:37:5: runtime error: invalid release
//! stdout:
//| before
//| after the first del: 16
// The test of a `del` reads the address of its pointer, and not the allocation that the
// pointer came from (D10.9 rule 4). Two allocations of 16 bytes stand side by side, so the
// address one after the last byte of the first is the start of the second. A trusted module
// forges an owner of that address here. Its `del` releases the second allocation: the live
// bytes go down by 16, and the `del` writes no error. The `del` of the owner that `new` gave
// for the second allocation is then a second release, the runtime error `invalid release`.
// Only a trusted module makes such an owner (D9.8). The interpreter reports the first `del`
// (spec/ownership-design.md 3.5), and test/fir_run_test.py holds that difference.
// D9.8, D10.3, D10.6, D10.9
import extern;
import std.heap;

// C: oracle_pass, test/lang/ffi/oracle.c.
// gives: p itself, which is no new allocation; this declaration says `own` to make an owner of
// the address after an allocation. writes: nothing. keeps: no pointer. calls: no fort
// function. frees: nothing.
extern fn oracle_pass(u8* p) u8 mut* own;

// C call oracle_pass: after.ptr, the address one after the last byte of `first`, which the
// empty span first[16..16] names (D9.8 rule 2: a bound of 0 bytes); C reads no byte.
fn main() i32 {
    u8 mut@ own first = new(u8, 16);
    u8 mut@ own second = new(u8, 16);
    u8@ after = first[16..16];
    u8 mut* own forged = oracle_pass(after.ptr);
    u64 live = heap.live_bytes();
    println("before");
    del(forged);
    println("after the first del: ", live - heap.live_bytes());
    del(second);
    println("after the second del");
    del(first);
    return 0;
}
